# SigRift environment configuration — template.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in real values, OR run scripts/quickstart.sh
# which generates a .env for you with strong random secrets.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   chmod 600 .env
#
# .env is gitignored. Never commit real secrets.

# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# UC_PROXY_SECRET is required in EVERY mode, development included: the
# shipped docker-compose.yml runs the bridge with BRIDGE_REQUIRE_PROXY=true,
# so without it the bridge refuses everything the gateway proxies to it
# (every dashboard and API call gets 403 "Direct access denied") and its
# healthcheck keeps it unhealthy, so the gateway never starts.
#
# In production (BRIDGE_ENV=production) the other three secrets below
# must be set too, all four to strong, random, unique values; the
# services refuse to start with defaults or empty values. docker-compose.yml
# passes BRIDGE_MFA_KEY, BRIDGE_AUDIT_KEY and UC_PROXY_SECRET to the bridge
# AND the gateway (same values on both); BRIDGE_MASTER_KEY, the admin API
# key, goes to the bridge only.
#
# BRIDGE_MFA_KEY must be a Fernet key: exactly 44 characters, 43 base64
# characters (A-Z a-z 0-9 - _ + /) then "=", encoding 32 random bytes.
# A 43-character token_urlsafe value is NOT valid — if you have one from
# an older setup, append "=" to it (same key bytes, so TOTP secrets
# already encrypted with it stay readable) instead of replacing it.
#
#   python3 -c "import base64, os; print(base64.urlsafe_b64encode(os.urandom(32)).decode())"
#   # (same as: from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; Fernet.generate_key())
BRIDGE_MFA_KEY=

# BRIDGE_KEYSTORE_KEY protects the protocol decryption keys imported in the
# console (Admin -> Crypto Keys) that the bridge keeps in the database.
# Same format and command as BRIDGE_MFA_KEY: the urlsafe base64 of 32
# random bytes (a Fernet key). The installers generate it. Optional:
# without it imported keys stay in memory only and are lost at every
# restart. Bridge only. It is bound to the data: never change it once keys
# are stored (keys stored with another value stay unreadable).
BRIDGE_KEYSTORE_KEY=

# The other three are random tokens. Generate each with:
#
#   python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
BRIDGE_AUDIT_KEY=
BRIDGE_MASTER_KEY=
# Required in every mode (see above).
UC_PROXY_SECRET=

# Initial admin account. Password must be at least 12 characters.
# A password set here is used as is: there is no forced change at first
# login (only an auto-generated password forces one).
BRIDGE_ADMIN_USER=admin
BRIDGE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Environment ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# production | development | test
#   production  — strict security guards: CSRF on, HSTS on, Secure cookies
#   development — relaxed: CSRF off, session cookie not marked Secure,
#                 ?api_key= accepted, weak secrets only warned about.
#                 Use production for any non-loopback SIGRIFT_BIND.
#   test        — like development
BRIDGE_ENV=development

# ── Optional: external port on the host ──────────────────────────────
# UC_PORT=8444

# ── Docker Compose project ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Docker Compose project (volume and network names). The installers write
# it once; changing it points this folder at other or empty volumes.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=

# ── Optional: who can reach the dashboard ────────────────────────────
# Host address docker publishes the gateway on:
#   127.0.0.1 — this machine only (the default when unset)
#   0.0.0.0   — every IPv4 interface: the LAN, remote edge nodes
#   one address of this host (192.168.1.5, or [fd00::5] for IPv6) — that
#               address only; the host-network scanners (wifi-scan,
#               bt-scan), which use https://127.0.0.1, then need their
#               MLRF_BRIDGE_URL changed
# Not [::]: docker publishes it on IPv6 only, so IPv4 clients and the
# scanners could not connect. For IPv6 as well as IPv4, keep 0.0.0.0 and
# add a second ports entry ("[::]:8444:8444") in a compose override file
# (INSTALL.md, "Who can reach the dashboard").
# SIGRIFT_BIND=127.0.0.1

# ── Optional: gateway <-> bridge network ─────────────────────────────
# The bridge sits on an internal network shared with the gateway only,
# and trusts X-Forwarded-For from the gateway's fixed address there.
# Docker hands other containers (the bridge) addresses from the IP range
# only, so the gateway's address must be inside the subnet but OUTSIDE
# the range — otherwise the bridge can take it and the gateway fails
# to start with "Address already in use".
# The default is outside Docker's default address pools. If it clashes
# with a network on your host ("Pool overlaps with other one on this
# address space" — e.g. a second SigRift stack), change all three
# together; scripts/quickstart.sh and install-release.sh do that for you,
# picking a free /28 and recording it here.
# SIGRIFT_BACKEND_SUBNET=10.203.84.0/28
# SIGRIFT_BACKEND_IP_RANGE=10.203.84.8/29
# SIGRIFT_GATEWAY_BACKEND_IP=10.203.84.2

# ── Optional: session binding ────────────────────────────────────────
# strict | ip | warn | none
#   strict — bind session to client IP + User-Agent (most restrictive)
#   ip     — bind to client IP only
#   warn   — log mismatches but don't reject
#   none   — no binding (the default — tokens replay from any IP, best
#            for mobile/NAT/VPN clients; see SECURITY.md for tradeoffs)
#
# Can also be toggled at runtime via the admin dashboard → System tab
# → Session Binding. Admin-UI changes persist but require a gateway
# container restart to apply (the gateway reads this var at startup).
# BRIDGE_SESSION_BINDING=none

# ── Optional: Content-Security-Policy (diagnosis only) ───────────────
# strict | report-only | disabled — the gateway's policy mode.
#   strict      — scripts only from the dashboard's own files (the
#                 default; an unknown value is strict too)
#   report-only — the browser runs what the policy would refuse and logs
#                 each violation to its developer console
#   disabled    — no policy at all
# report-only and disabled remove the protection against injected
# markup: set one only while diagnosing a page, then remove it.
# UC_CSP_MODE=strict

# ── Optional: node API keys ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A NODE/SENSOR API key without a node_id is bound to the first node it
# reports for (heartbeat, detection batch, ...); after that it is refused
# (403) for any other node. Use one key per node, or create the key with
# its node_id. 1 keeps unbound keys unbound — any node — as before; it is
# passed to the bridge and the gateway, which both enforce the binding.
# BRIDGE_ALLOW_UNBOUND_NODE_KEYS=

# ── Optional: TLS (production) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Paths are inside the gateway container; mount them via compose.
# Without these, scripts/quickstart.sh and install-release.sh generate a
# self-signed cert covering this host's names and addresses; add more
# names/IPs on their command line (not here), e.g.
#   SIGRIFT_TLS_SANS=sigrift.example.org,203.0.113.7 scripts/quickstart.sh
# UC_TLS_CERT=/etc/letsencrypt/live/bridge.example.com/fullchain.pem
# UC_TLS_KEY=/etc/letsencrypt/live/bridge.example.com/privkey.pem
